{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Morning came softly, without the usual clang of the harbor bell. Mira woke in the cottage on Tern Street, the one with the blue shutters they had painted together the summer Tobias broke his wrist. Sunlight lay across the quilt in long gold bars. Downstairs, the kettle was already singing. She found him at the window in his shirtsleeves, watching the boats rock at their moorings. \"No one's going out,\" he said, not troubled, only curious. \"There's no need,\" she told him, and knew it was true the moment she said it. Nets hung drying that would never be wet again. The fishmonger's cart stood empty, heaped instead with wildflowers someone had gathered at dawn. They walked the cliff path after breakfast, past the church where the bell rope swayed untouched, past the little graveyard whose stones had gone smooth and blank as river pebbles. At the lighthouse Tobias paused, one hand on the cool whitewashed wall. For forty years he had climbed those steps every evening, trimming wicks, polishing glass, counting ships home. Now the door stood open and the lamp room was full of nothing but light. \"Do you miss it?\" Mira asked. He considered the question honestly, the way he considered everything. \"I thought I would,\" he said. \"But everyone I was watching for is already here.\" Below them the gulls wheeled and settled. Children ran shrieking along the sand, and among them, laughing louder than anyone, were the twins from Gull Point, small again for the pleasure of it. Mira leaned against her husband's shoulder, and the sea breathed in, and out, and in."}
